The issue with Tiled Rendering is that the Pixel shading and ROP logic is done on a Per Tile basis. 

There is generally a 1 : 1 match between a Core and a Tile. 

Trouble is, in heavily unbalanced scenes, most of the work could be concentrated in a few Tiles and thus the remaining cores would be sitting idle.

------

To avoid this problem, they are keeping tabs (counters) on a Per Tile basis during Binning. It could be a list of triangles, an addition of compiler added weight of the Pixel shader etc.

After Binning, Tiles are sorted based on decreasing weight. The Top 'n' Tiles are resplit into smaller Tiles before distributing if its noticed that some Cores will be sitting idle.

To Split a Tile, one can send the same Tile to different Cores and with smaller scissors. 


They each go through the same list of Primitives and ignore those that fail the Rasterizer's Scissor test

